{"reference":"Revelation 17","book":"Revelation","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Babylon and Imperial Power","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Within Revelation’s wider vision, “Babylon” probably points to Rome in the first-century setting while also drawing on biblical Babylon imagery to criticize imperial city-power. The book’s concern is broader than identifying one emperor as the complete key: Roman political power, civic religion, and economy form important background. Its imagery also repeatedly reworks Israel’s Scriptures, including Daniel, Ezekiel, Isaiah, Exodus, Zechariah, Genesis, and Psalms.","verse_refs":["Revelation 10:1-11","Revelation 11:1-19","Revelation 12:1-17","Revelation 13:1-18","Revelation 14:1-20","Revelation 15:1-8","Revelation 16:1-21","Revelation 18:1-24","Revelation 19:1-21","Revelation 1:1-20","Revelation 20:1-15","Revelation 21:1-27","Revelation 22:1-21","Revelation 2:1-29","Revelation 3:1-22","Revelation 4:1-11","Revelation 5:1-14","Revelation 6:1-17","Revelation 7:1-17","Revelation 8:1-13","Revelation 9:1-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_revelation_017_d3550ba8b4735d2eb375649d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}